---
intent: >
  Collapse provisionBrain from a seven-step pipeline (with brainPath
  parameter, retired step builders for brain root and four entry-point
  artifacts, legacy obsidian-key migration, and a literal `<BRAIN_PATH>`
  sentinel) to a two-step pipeline whose only outputs are the
  brain.aide scaffold step and the MCP entry plan step. Drop the
  brainPath parameter at every layer (orchestrator, the bundled
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper, every step builder). The bundled
  template emits YAML null at the unwired path slot of
  `mcpServerConfig.args`; the null propagates verbatim through
  parseBrainAide and interpolateArgs into the MCP step's prescription.
  Delete the five retired step builders, the legacy obsidian-key
  migration code in the MCP step, the `resolveBrainAideBody` helper
  and its consumers, the `BRAIN_ROOT_DIRS` constant, and the
  `brainRootExists` helper. Forbid `PATH_SLOT_INDEX` constants,
  `resolveBrainRoot` helpers, and any other arg-introspection code.
  Update the `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` config-section body bytes to
  describe the new flow: identify YAML null at the path slot, fill
  via direct edit, run sync, then seed the four entry-point artifacts
  via the brain's MCP write tool by reading the seed-section bytes
  through parseBrainAide's typed keys. Drop the `brainPath` argument
  at the one external caller site (`src/service/install/index.ts`)
  so this module's two-argument signature compiles end-to-end.
---

## Project Structure

This plan governs the `provisionBrain/` module only. After execution,
the tree below is the complete state of the module — every file
listed exists; nothing else does. Files marked MODIFIED are rewritten
in place; nothing is added or deleted at the file level.

```
src/service/install/provisionBrain/
├── .aide                                  ← intent spec (existing — refreshed Stage 1+3, not touched here)
├── plan.aide                              ← THIS plan (rewritten this cycle, replacing the stale 6-step variant)
├── todo.aide                              ← prior-cycle carry-forward (informational; not touched by this plan)
├── index.ts                               ← orchestrator + helpers — MODIFIED
│   ├── provisionBrain(projectRoot: string, mcpConfigPath: string): Promise<InitStep[]>
│   │     — orchestrator. Returns exactly TWO InitStep records in fixed order:
│   │       [brainAideStep, mcpStep]. No `brainPath` parameter, no
│   │       `effectiveBrainPath` derivation, no slot-position introspection.
│   ├── buildBrainAideStep(projectRoot: string): Promise<InitStep>
│   │     — presence-only idempotency on `.aide/config/brain.aide`. Absent
│   │       → `would-create` with the bundled template bytes from
│   │       `obsidianBrainAideTemplate()` (called with no arguments).
│   │       Present → `exists` with no `content` field. Never returns
│   │       `would-overwrite` for any path under `.aide/config/`.
│   ├── buildBrainMcpStep(projectRoot: string, brainAideStep: InitStep, mcpConfigPath: string): Promise<InitStep>
│   │     — derives the prescription from the just-scaffolded (or
│   │       on-disk) brain.aide via `resolveBrainAideConfig`, then
│   │       handles exactly four on-disk states of `.mcp.json`:
│   │       (1) file absent or empty → `would-create`;
│   │       (2) `mcpServers.brain` absent → `would-create`;
│   │       (3) `mcpServers.brain` present and matches → `exists`;
│   │       (4) `mcpServers.brain` present and differs → `would-overwrite`;
│   │       malformed JSON → `would-create` with `configMalformed: true`.
│   │       Args carrying `null` entries from a partially-wired
│   │       brain.aide propagate verbatim into `prescription.entry.args`;
│   │       the comparison treats null structurally (`null === null`,
│   │       `null === "string"` false).
│   ├── resolveBrainAideConfig(projectRoot: string, brainAideStep: InitStep): Promise<{ command: string; args: (string | null)[] } | null>
│   │     — single source of the derived MCP entry. Same in-memory-vs-on-disk
│   │       dispatch as before: `would-create` + `content` defined →
│   │       `parseBrainAideFromString(content)`; otherwise → `parseBrainAide(projectRoot)`.
│   │       On non-ok parse → `null`. On `kind === "ok"` → returns
│   │       `{ command, args }` from `interpolateArgs({ name, mcpServerConfig })`,
│   │       with `args` typed `(string | null)[]` (the broadened parser
│   │       contract preserves null entries verbatim).
│   ├── exists(path: string): Promise<boolean>                          ← unchanged
│   └── safeReadFile(path: string): Promise<string>                     ← unchanged
└── obsidianBrainAideTemplate/
    ├── index.ts                           ← bundled template — MODIFIED
    │   └── obsidianBrainAideTemplate(): string
    │         — no parameters. Emits the canonical six-section brain.aide
    │           bytes with `mcpServerConfig.args` ending in a bare-dash
    │           YAML null line (parses to JS `null`) at the path slot.
    │           POSIX shape: `["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null]`. Win32 shape:
    │           `["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null]`. The literal
    │           token `<BRAIN_PATH>` appears nowhere in the output.
    │           The `<!-- aide-config-start -->` body section carries
    │           prose describing (a) YAML null at `args[3]` (win32 shape;
    │           the bundled Obsidian default's path slot) as the
    │           unwired-state signal, and (b) the post-sync entry-point-
    │           artifact seeding flow: agent reads the four seed sections
    │           from brain.aide via parseBrainAide's typed keys
    │           (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
    │           `researchIndex`) and writes each at its named path
    │           (`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`, `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`,
    │           `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, `research/research.md`)
    │           via `mcp__brain__write_note`.
    └── index.test.ts                      ← bundled template tests — MODIFIED
          — every `getTemplate(brainPath, ...)` call drops the brainPath
            argument; `getTemplate()` and `getTemplate(undefined, "win32")`
            are the only call shapes. Every assertion against `<BRAIN_PATH>`
            is removed; new assertions check that args[1] (POSIX) /
            args[3] (win32) parse as JS `null` strictly. The config-section
            assertion suite asserts the new prose mentioning YAML null
            and the entry-point seeding step.
```

The module's test entry point at `index.test.ts` lives directly under
`provisionBrain/` (already present); it is rewritten to the
two-step contract — see Step 2 for the test matrix.

## Prerequisites

The following work must complete before this plan's steps run.

- `src/service/parseBrainAide` exports the broadened
  `mcpServerConfig.args: (string | null)[]` typed contract from both
  `parseBrainAide` and `parseBrainAideFromString` (ok-variant returns).
  `interpolateArgs` accepts the broadened args type and forwards null
  entries verbatim — every null in the input lands at the same index in
  the output, with no replace call running on null. The shared types in
  `src/types/index.ts` (`BrainAideMcpServerConfig.args`,
  `McpPrescription.entry.args`, `McpServerEntry.args`) are widened to
  `(string | null)[]` so the parser's typed result, the `interpolateArgs`
  return value, and the prescription shape agree at compile time.
  This module forwards the args array into `prescription.entry.args`
  without copying or coercion; a type mismatch at any of these
  boundaries would prevent this plan's edits from compiling.

- The shared `InitStep` and `InitStepStatus` shapes in `src/types/index.ts`
  remain unchanged — the four planning statuses (`would-create`,
  `would-overwrite`, `would-skip`, `exists`) cover everything this
  module emits. No new status is needed; `would-skip` is not used by
  this module under the new contract (the dependent artifact-step
  builders that previously used it are gone).

- `src/service/install/index.ts` continues to call this module from the
  `brainPath !== undefined` branch with three arguments; that call site
  is updated in this plan (Step 3) to drop the third argument so the
  module's new two-argument signature compiles end-to-end.

## Plan

### 1. Rewrite the bundled template helper to take no arguments and emit YAML null at the path slot

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`,
`coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`,
`coding-playbook/foundations/anti-patterns`

The bundled template is the single source of launcher bytes and all
six body section bytes. This step rewrites it to take no arguments
and to emit YAML null at the path slot, plus updates the
`<!-- aide-config-start -->` body section prose to describe the new
flow (identify-null → ask → edit → sync → seed-entry-points). Helper
and its tests change together — they are coupled because the test
suite asserts directly on the helper's output bytes, and a parameter
rename plus YAML emission change cannot be observed in isolation.

- [x] 1a. Change the helper signature in
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts` from
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate(brainPath?: string): string` to
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate(): string`. Remove the `pathArg` local
  variable, the `<BRAIN_PATH>` fallback expression, and every
  `${pathArg}` interpolation in the emitted YAML. Drop every
  reference to a `brainPath` parameter from JSDoc and any inline
  comments.

- [x] 1b. Update the `mcpServerConfig` YAML emission in both platform
  branches so the `args:` block ends with a bare-dash line that YAML
  parses to JS `null`. Pick one form consistently (a bare `-` line
  with no scalar after the dash, indented to match the surrounding
  `args:` block). Each platform's args list ends with that
  null-emitting line:
  - POSIX branch: `'@bitbonsai/mcpvault'` then a bare-dash null line.
    Parsed `args` is `["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null]`.
  - Win32 branch: `'/c'`, `'npx'`, `'@bitbonsai/mcpvault'`, then a
    bare-dash null line. Parsed `args` is
    `["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null]`.

  No quoted empty string `''`, no literal `<BRAIN_PATH>`, no
  `${rootPath}` interpolation, no other in-band string the package
  recognizes — the unwired-slot signal is YAML null, full stop.

- [x] 1c. Rewrite the bytes between `<!-- aide-config-start -->` and
  `<!-- aide-config-end -->` so the prose describes (i) the
  unwired-state signal as YAML null at `args[3]` for the bundled
  Obsidian default (the win32 shape is the canonical reference;
  POSIX's path slot is at `args[1]` but the integration prose names
  the win32 index because the bundled template's documented arg
  position is `args[3]`); (ii) the five-step wiring flow under the
  new contract: read brain.aide, decide the target path
  (`$ARGUMENTS` non-empty / null at args[3] / real path already
  there), edit `brain.aide` to replace the null with the absolute
  path, run sync, then SEED the four entry-point artifacts before
  emitting the restart message. The seed sub-step is new in this
  prose: the agent (a) reads brain.aide via the brain's read tool;
  (b) extracts the four seed-section bytes via parseBrainAide's
  typed keys (`playbookIndex` → `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`,
  `studyPlaybook` → `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`,
  `updatePlaybook` → `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`,
  `researchIndex` → `research/research.md`); (c) writes each
  artifact via `mcp__brain__write_note` at the path the prose names.
  The presence-check + re-seed loop (skip already-existing artifacts,
  write the missing ones) owns missing-artifact recovery on
  subsequent `/aide:brain config` runs. After seeding, the prose
  emits the restart message verbatim.

  Storage-agnostic vocabulary applies to this prose's package-authored
  text outside the user-owned-section-bytes carve-out boundary, but
  the carve-out lets the bundled Obsidian default's prose mention
  Obsidian by name and reference `mcp__brain__write_note` (the
  Obsidian-flavored write tool) — the spec explicitly carves the
  bundled template's body section bytes out of the storage-agnostic
  rule. The forbidden token `<BRAIN_PATH>` does NOT carve out; it is
  the retired sentinel and must not appear anywhere in the new
  prose.

- [x] 1d. Rewrite the JSDoc on `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` to
  document (i) the no-argument signature; (ii) the path slot
  carrying YAML null in every emission, with the integration's
  `config` body section telling the agent what `args[3]` means for
