### 2. Rewrite the orchestrator to two steps; delete every retired step builder, helper, and constant; rewrite the test suite

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`,
`coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`,
`coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`,
`coding-playbook/foundations/anti-patterns`

The orchestrator at `src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.ts`
shrinks dramatically. Two step builders survive
(`buildBrainAideStep`, `buildBrainMcpStep`), one helper survives
(`resolveBrainAideConfig`, with its return type widened to
`(string | null)[]`), and two utility helpers survive (`exists`,
`safeReadFile`). Everything else is deleted. The test suite is
rewritten to the two-step contract: every test that calls
`provisionBrain(projectRoot, brainPath, mcpPath)` becomes
`provisionBrain(projectRoot, mcpPath)`; tests for the four
artifact-step builders, the brain-root step, the `<BRAIN_PATH>`
sentinel, and the legacy obsidian-key migration are removed; new
tests cover the two-step shape, YAML-null propagation, the MCP
step's four branches, and the directory-wide ownership rule on
`.aide/config/`.

This is one delegation unit because every sub-step touches the
orchestrator's shape together: deleting a step builder shrinks the
orchestrator; deleting a helper requires deleting its consumers;
rewriting the test suite cannot stand alone if the production code
still has the old shape.

- [x] 2a. In `src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.ts`, delete
  the following code surfaces entirely:
  - The `buildBrainRootStep` function (no consumer remains; the
    brain root directory step is gone from this module's pipeline).
  - The `buildPlaybookStep` function.
  - The `buildStudyPlaybookStep` function.
  - The `buildUpdatePlaybookStep` function.
  - The `buildResearchStep` function.
  - The `resolveBrainAideBody` helper (no consumer remains after
    the four artifact-step builders are gone).
  - The `BRAIN_ROOT_DIRS` constant (no consumer remains).
  - The `brainRootExists` helper (no consumer remains).
  - The `readdir` import from `node:fs/promises` (no consumer
    remains after `brainRootExists` is gone). Verify by Grep that
    `readdir` is unreferenced after the deletions and remove it.

  Forbidden additions (must NEVER appear in this file under any
  rename): a `PATH_SLOT_INDEX` constant, a `BRAIN_ROOT_ARG`
  constant, a `VAULT_PATH_INDEX` constant, a `resolveBrainRoot`
  helper, an `extractBrainPath` helper, a `getBrainRoot` helper, a
  `pickPathSlot` helper, a `readBrainRootFromArgs` helper, an
  `argSlots` reference, any `args[N]` switch on a named integer
  N, any matcher recognizing token-shaped strings at a specific
  index, any code path that reads `mcpServerConfig.args[N]` for any
  reason other than byte-faithful forwarding through interpolateArgs.
  This is the structural anti-regression rule the spec pins
  (outcomes.undesired item on arg-slot introspection).

- [x] 2b. Rewrite the `provisionBrain` orchestrator export. The new
  signature is
  `provisionBrain(projectRoot: string, mcpConfigPath: string): Promise<InitStep[]>`
  — no `brainPath` parameter, no `effectiveBrainPath` derivation,
  no fallback string. The body has exactly three statements:
  - `const brainAideStep = await buildBrainAideStep(projectRoot);`
  - `const mcpStep = await buildBrainMcpStep(projectRoot, brainAideStep, mcpConfigPath);`
  - `return [brainAideStep, mcpStep];`

  Update the function's JSDoc to (i) document the two-argument
  signature; (ii) state that the returned array length is exactly
  2 in fixed order (brain config first, MCP second); (iii)
  describe the YAML-null contract — the bundled template emits
