  brain root directory are NOT this module's responsibility — they
  are seeded by the integration's `aide-config` body section
  prose at first `/aide:brain config` via the brain's MCP write
  tool, after the user fills the null slot and runs sync; (v)
  drop every `<BRAIN_PATH>` reference, every reference to legacy
  obsidian-key migration, every reference to `brainPath`, every
  reference to a brain root directory step, every reference to
  artifact-step builders.

- [x] 2c. Update `buildBrainAideStep` to accept only
  `projectRoot: string` (drop the `brainPath: string | undefined`
  parameter). The body calls `obsidianBrainAideTemplate()` with
  no arguments. The seed-semantic idempotency rule is unchanged:
  presence at `.aide/config/brain.aide` returns `exists` with no
  `content` field; absence returns `would-create` with the bundled
  template bytes as `content`. The JSDoc loses every reference to
  the optional `brainPath` parameter and the `<BRAIN_PATH>`
  sentinel; the directory-wide-ownership-of-`.aide/config/`
  paragraph stays as-is.

- [x] 2d. Update `resolveBrainAideConfig` to declare its return
  type as
  `Promise<{ command: string; args: (string | null)[] } | null>`.
  The body change is a single type annotation lift — the call to
  `interpolateArgs({ name, mcpServerConfig })` already returns
  `(string | null)[]` under the prerequisite-broadened parser
  contract; the return-type annotation widens to match. Update
  the JSDoc to mention that `args` may carry null entries
  forwarded verbatim from the on-disk or in-memory brain.aide,
  and that the helper does not introspect or substitute null at
  any index.

- [x] 2e. Rewrite `buildBrainMcpStep` to handle exactly four
  branches plus the malformed-JSON branch:
  - Compute `expectedEntry = await resolveBrainAideConfig(projectRoot, brainAideStep)`
    once at the top.
  - Build the `prescription` as
    `{ key: "brain", entry: expectedEntry ?? { command: "", args: [] } }`.
    The empty-array fallback type-checks as `(string | null)[]`.
  - Read the existing `.mcp.json` via `safeReadFile(mcpConfigPath)`.
  - **No file** (empty string from `safeReadFile`) →
    `{ name: "MCP config (brain)", status: "would-create", category: "mcp", filePath: mcpConfigPath, prescription }`.
  - **Malformed JSON** (the `JSON.parse` catch branch) →
    `{ name: "MCP config (brain)", status: "would-create", category: "mcp", filePath: mcpConfigPath, prescription, configMalformed: true }`.
  - **`mcpServers.brain` absent** → same shape as the no-file
    branch (`would-create`).
  - **`mcpServers.brain` present** → element-by-element comparison
    of `existing_entry.command` against `prescription.entry.command`,
    plus deep array equality on `args` (length match plus
    element-by-element `===`). When all elements match → `exists`
    with no `content` and no `prescription` fields; when anything
    differs → `would-overwrite` with the prescription set.

  Element-by-element `===` correctly handles `null`: `null === null`
  is `true`, `null === "string"` is `false`, so a previously-null
  on-disk arg compared against a now-string prescription arg
  reports drift and vice versa. No additional null-handling code
  is needed.

  Forbidden code paths inside `buildBrainMcpStep` (the structural
  anti-regression):
  - The `const hasObsidian = "obsidian" in servers` line — gone.
  - The `if (hasBrain && hasObsidian)` transitional both-keys
    branch — gone.
  - The `if (hasObsidian)` legacy-only branch — gone.
  - Any introspection of `args[N]` for any N other than
    byte-faithful element-by-element comparison.
  - Any defensive substitution of null in `prescription.entry.args`
    (no replacing null with empty string, no dropping null entries,
    no converting null to a literal-string sentinel).

  Update the JSDoc to enumerate the four branches plus malformed-JSON.
  Drop every reference to legacy obsidian-key migration, the
  `hasObsidian` inspection, the both-keys transitional branch, and
  the `<BRAIN_PATH>` sentinel. Add a sentence stating that
  `prescription.entry.args` may contain `null` entries when the
  brain.aide path slot is YAML null, and that the step emits the
  same shape regardless — sync (downstream) refuses to write
  null-bearing args, so a partially-wired brain configuration
  cannot silently land in `.mcp.json`.

- [x] 2f. Rewrite `src/service/install/provisionBrain/index.test.ts`
  to the two-step contract. Delete the following test blocks
  outright:
  - The seven `name` assertions in test `5a` that referenced step
    indices 1–5 (Brain root directories, Playbook, Study-playbook,
