## Decisions

- **provisionBrain shrinks to exactly two InitStep records — the
  brain.aide scaffold step first, the MCP entry plan step second
  — and nothing else.** The spec is explicit (Strategy: "Two
  planning steps in fixed order"; outcomes.desired enumerates
  exactly those two); the rework was driven by a structural
  failure mode the previous seven-step shape carried (the
  PATH_SLOT_INDEX / resolveBrainRoot proposal). The four
  entry-point artifacts and the brain root directory move
  entirely to the integration's `aide-config` body section
  prose, where slot-semantics, file-path, and write-tool-name
  knowledge ALL live in user-owned text. provisionBrain becomes
  backend-blind at the slot level. Restoring any of the five
  retired step builders (`buildBrainRootStep`, `buildPlaybookStep`,
  `buildStudyPlaybookStep`, `buildUpdatePlaybookStep`,
  `buildResearchStep`) under any rename is the structural
  regression this spec forbids — it is the same shape regardless
  of identifier (`seedPlaybook`, `writePlaybookArtifact`,
  `scaffoldPlaybookIndex`, `seedBrainRoot`, `ensureBrainRoot`,
  etc. all encode the same regression).

- **NO `PATH_SLOT_INDEX` constant, NO `resolveBrainRoot` helper,
  NO arg-index introspection of any kind.** The handoff document
  pinned this as the load-bearing rule that drove the rework.
  The previous architect's plan introduced both as a
  bounded-concession workaround for the seven-step shape; the
  user explicitly rejected that and chose the structural-fix
  alternative (provisionBrain shrinks; the integration prose
  owns the slot semantics). This plan forbids them by name AND
  by shape — any module-local constant whose value is an
  `args` index, any helper whose body reads `args[N]` for any
  named integer N, any switch on a slot position, any matcher
  recognizing token-shaped strings at a specific index, any
  `argSlots` frontmatter field. Slot semantics live exclusively
  in the per-integration `config` body section, owned by the
  user; the package never inspects which slot is which. This
  decision is the architect-level rule that drives every step
  in the plan.

- **The unwired-slot signal is YAML null, not a literal-string
  sentinel.** `mcpServerConfig.args` is typed `(string | null)[]`,
  the typing is the contract, and YAML null is the explicit
  structural unwired-slot signal carried by the schema itself.
  The retired `<BRAIN_PATH>` literal-sentinel design produced
  the byte-for-byte-agreement-passes-drift-check silent-failure
  bug confirmed in `D:\Code\Me\aide-fresh` (handoff Stage 1
  notes); structural null closes that trap at the schema layer.
  No package code recognizes a string the template chose as the
  "unwired" marker; the typed schema carries the signal directly.
  Sync's null-refusal contract at `.mcp.json` write time is the
  load-bearing safety net that catches partially-wired
  configurations loudly.

- **The `<!-- aide-config-start -->` body bytes get rewritten in
  this plan rather than deferred.** The user-owned-section-bytes
  carve-out (root spec) permits the bundled obsidian-flavored
  default to ship package-authored prose inside the seed
  sections. The current config-section prose references the
  retired `<BRAIN_PATH>` literal as the unwired-state signal;
  under the new contract that signal is YAML null. Leaving the
  prose unchanged would ship a bundled default whose `config`
  body section instructs the agent to look for a string sentinel
  that never lands, breaking the wiring flow on first run.
  Rewriting the prose in this plan keeps the bundled template's
  install-time and agent-runtime contracts coherent. The new
  prose ALSO carries the entry-point-artifact-seeding step
  (read seed sections via parseBrainAide's typed keys, write
  via the brain's MCP write tool), which is the load-bearing
  hand-off that lets the four artifacts move out of
  provisionBrain's pipeline without losing the install-time
  recovery story.

- **`brainPath` is removed entirely from every signature in this
  module — there is no two-argument-vs-three-argument
  compatibility shim, no rest-parameter trick, no overload set.**
  Adding any compatibility shim would re-couple the install
  pipeline to a caller-supplied path and break the
  cli/init-is-brain-agnostic invariant on the next refactor. The
  one external caller (`service/install/index.ts`) is updated in
  Step 3 to drop the third argument; the install service's own
  `brainPath` parameter on the `init` function stays intact (it
  is part of `aide_init`'s public input schema, used by the
  agent to confirm or override discovered brain hints) but is
  no longer forwarded into provisionBrain — that is acceptable
  because provisionBrain's spec is explicit that the bundled
  template emits YAML null regardless of caller input.

- **Legacy `obsidian`-key migration is removed in one pass, not
  gated behind a transitional read path.** The handoff is
  explicit: drop legacy migration entirely. There is no
  `obsidian`-to-`brain` rename heuristic, no warning log, no
  "we noticed you have an obsidian key" branch. The user's
  `.mcp.json` may carry an `obsidian` key indefinitely from a
  pre-rework install; that key is the user's, not this package's,
  and sync's narrow contract (write only `mcpServers.brain`)
  preserves it. If the user wants the orphan obsidian key gone
  they remove it themselves; the package no longer offers
  migration. The corresponding storage-agnostic-vocabulary
  carve-out for the legacy obsidian-key migration code was
  dropped in the root spec's #19 carve-outs list.

- **`would-skip` is no longer used by this module.** The retired
  step builders used `would-skip` to signal both the
  parse-failure case and (in the prior plan) the unwired-path
  case. Both consumers are gone. The brain.aide step uses
  `exists` / `would-create`; the MCP step uses `exists` /
  `would-create` / `would-overwrite` plus the malformed-JSON
  `configMalformed: true` flag; neither emits `would-skip`. The
  `InitStepStatus` union still carries `would-skip` for other
  modules' use.

- **`buildBrainMcpStep` reuses element-by-element `===` for null
  comparison.** The current `args.every((a, i) => a === prescription.entry.args[i])`
  expression handles `null` correctly without code change:
  `null === null` is `true`, `null === "string"` is `false`. A
  `Boolean(...)` truthiness check or a `JSON.stringify` round-trip
  comparison would silently drop drift between null and empty
  string and is forbidden. The strict-equality semantics of `===`
  is the correct shape; the spec's outcomes.undesired item on
  defensive null-replacement covers this regression class.

- **Tests assert path-slot null with strict `null` equality
  (`expect(x).toBeNull()` or `toEqual` with literal `null`)
  rather than truthiness or empty-string equality.** The spec is
  explicit (outcomes.undesired: "Substituting any other shape
  for the YAML null at any unwired slot of `mcpServerConfig.args`").
  A truthiness check would silently pass if the path slot were
  coerced to empty string; a `null` strict-equality check
  catches that regression. The same applies to the
  `args[i] !== null` versus `Boolean(args[i])` distinction in
  every test expectation.

- **`makeSixSectionBrainAide` is updated to handle a null array
  element rather than requiring tests to construct the YAML
  bytes manually.** Two options were considered: (a) update the
  fixture helper so a JS `null` at an args index emits a
  bare-dash YAML line; (b) keep the current string-only fixture
  and have tests pass a manually-built array containing a literal
  `"null"` string at the path slot (which the YAML parser would
  parse as the string `"null"`, not as YAML null). Option (a)
  preserves the test-fixture-as-typed-shape contract (tests pass
  the same JS values they want to assert on); option (b) breaks
  it (tests pass a string, the parser returns a different
  string, the assertion fails). Option (a) is the right shape;
  the fixture helper's line-construction expression takes a
  per-element conditional that emits `    -` (bare dash) for JS
  `null` and `    - ${a}` for any string.

- **Test for `MCP step is exists when both sides carry null at
  the path slot` is the regression guard against double-null
  drift collapsing to `would-overwrite` accidentally.** The
  scenario: cold install, brain.aide carries null at args[1],
  prior `.mcp.json` brain entry also carries null at args[1].
  The MCP step compares args element-by-element; null === null
  is true; the step reports `exists`. This is correct per the
  spec — the user has not yet wired the brain, both sides agree
  on the null, the step plans no change. Sync's null-refusal at
  write time catches the partially-wired state when the agent
  would actually try to write; the planning surface itself is
  benign here. The test exists to document and guard this
  intentional shape; without it, a refactor that swaps the
  comparison for a `JSON.stringify` round-trip (for example)
  would silently regress the `exists` branch into `would-overwrite`.

- **Test for `MCP step is would-overwrite when brain.aide is
  wired but .mcp.json is still null` is the recovery-shape
  regression guard.** The scenario: user filled brain.aide's
  null slot with a real path but never re-ran sync. brain.aide
  carries `["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "/real/path"]`; `.mcp.json`
  brain entry carries `["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null]`. The MCP
  step compares args element-by-element; `"/real/path" === null`
  is false; the step reports `would-overwrite` with the wired
  prescription. This is the recovery-by-sync shape that lets a
  partially-wired user re-converge by running sync; without the
  test, a refactor that defensively coerced one side or the
  other would silently mask the recovery path.

- **No new helpers in this scope.** The plan reuses every
  surviving helper (`exists`, `safeReadFile`, `resolveBrainAideConfig`)
  and the surviving step builders. No new orchestrator-level
  helper is needed — the change is helper deletion, not
  addition. Adding a helper purely to "branch on the parse
  result" or "compose the prescription" would violate the
  playbook's "extract helpers into subfolders only when they
  have independent reasons to exist" rule. The
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper folder stays as the only
  subfolder; it is the bundled-template-content carve-out from
  the storage-agnostic naming rule.

- **The `obsidianBrainAideTemplate/` folder name stays as-is.**
  The spec carves this folder name out as the only allowed
  storage-specific identifier in this module — it describes
  bundled template content, not a code-level dispatch axis.
  Renaming it (e.g. to `defaultBrainAideTemplate/`) is its own
  refactor across the install service and is outside this
  plan's scope.

- **The single external caller's invocation site is updated in
  this plan rather than treated as a follow-up.** The previous
  cycle's plan deferred fixing `service/install/index.ts` to a
  follow-up plan, accepting the compile error as a "surface
  signal" the follow-up needs to land. That approach forces the
  build phase to land a known-broken intermediate state where
  `provisionBrain`'s tests pass but the workspace `tsc` fails.
  The fix is one-line and has no design surface; landing it in
  this plan keeps the workspace compiling end-to-end and lets
  the orchestrator declare the cycle done with a green
  project-wide test suite. The scope expansion is bounded — the
  edit is mechanical, the test surface is unchanged, and the
  install service's own spec does not need amending (its
  `brainPath` parameter on `init`'s signature stays as part of
  `aide_init`'s public input schema; only the forwarded
  argument into provisionBrain drops).

- **Verification step (Step 4) is a Grep + hand-trace pass, not
  a new test.** The two structural invariants the spec pins
  (two-step contract, no arg-introspection) are best caught at
  the source-code level by Grep against the forbidden tokens —
  unit tests can be made to pass with a regression in place if
  the test was written before the regression was discovered.
  Step 4d's Grep against `PATH_SLOT_INDEX|resolveBrainRoot|...`
  catches structural regressions the unit tests might miss
  (a hidden constant in a comment block, a helper added in a
  refactor that survived test deletion). The hand-trace
  sub-steps (4a/4b/4c) walk the spec's worked Good-examples to
  catch behavioral drift that compiles-and-passes-tests but
  diverges from the documented shape.

- **The plan does not modify the install service's `aide_init`
  caller-shape (`brainPath` parameter on `init`).** The
  `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` flow passes `brainPath`
  into `init`, which previously forwarded it into
  `provisionBrain`. Step 3 drops the forwarding but keeps the
  parameter on `init`'s signature so the install service's own
  spec and its public input schema do not break. The wider
  question of whether `aide_init` should retire the
  `brainPath` input entirely (since no consumer in the new
  contract reads it) is the install service's concern,
  tracked in the install service's own spec, not this plan.
